WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.176 --> 00:00:11.583
SAMANTHA: Um. so. the light that we're
seeing now from

2
00:00:11.606 --> 00:00:12.832
the Horsehead Nebula

3
00:00:12.932 --> 00:00:16.015
started its journey
a thousand years ago.

4
00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.390
We're actually looking at the past.

5
00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:22.527
N-No time machine needed.
just a telescope.

6
00:00:22.626 --> 00:00:25.329
You can see the most
amazing things with it.

7
00:00:25.463 --> 00:00:27.931
a-and you never have
to talk to anybody at all.

8
00:00:28.032 --> 00:00:30.868
Not that I don't want to talk with you.

9
00:00:30.969 --> 00:00:32.871
I-I mean. I'm having a-a lovely night.

10
00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.573
I ju... (SIGHS)

11
00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.475
What I meant to say

12
00:00:37.576 --> 00:00:39.878
is that. um...

13
00:00:39.978 --> 00:00:41.646
it's nice spending time with someone

14
00:00:41.746 --> 00:00:46.320
who actually understands
how beautiful science can be.

15
00:00:49.353 --> 00:00:50.826
Ben?

16
00:00:53.491 --> 00:00:55.460
BRADLEY: Federal agents!

17
00:00:56.094 --> 00:00:57.606
Stop or we'll shoot!

18
00:00:57.707 --> 00:00:59.162
(GRUNTING)

19
00:01:06.103 --> 00:01:07.572
He's headed toward the residence!

20
00:01:09.873 --> 00:01:11.608
Take the shot.

21
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:16.581
Suspect down.

22
00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.103
Is the vice president secure?

23
00:01:19.478 --> 00:01:22.721
Repeat. is the vice president secure?

24
00:01:24.623 --> 00:01:26.790
♪ ♪

25
00:01:46.365 --> 00:01:54.365
Synced & corrected by -robtor-
www.addic7ed.com

26
00:01:55.776 --> 00:01:57.054
This is a joke. right?

27
00:01:57.155 --> 00:01:59.691
No. I assure you. it's not a joke.

28
00:02:00.006 --> 00:02:01.406
Maybe you're just not in on it.

29
00:02:01.507 --> 00:02:03.742
Mm. this is. uh. feeling
like I'm late for something.

30
00:02:03.843 --> 00:02:05.144
Am I late for something?

31
00:02:05.278 --> 00:02:06.945
No. you are just in time.

32
00:02:07.046 --> 00:02:09.447
Apparently. we're getting
a new member of the team.

33
00:02:09.548 --> 00:02:10.615
What?

34
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:12.251
KNIGHT: We have just been told.

35
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.389
and I quote.
that "this cell phone thingy

36
00:02:15.489 --> 00:02:17.156
will be working our cases with us."

37
00:02:17.256 --> 00:02:18.724
I told you.
it's not a "cell phone thingy."

38
00:02:18.824 --> 00:02:20.626
it's an A.I. chatbot.

39
00:02:20.727 --> 00:02:23.028
That's gonna be working
our cases with us?

40
00:02:23.128 --> 00:02:25.031
Yes. uh. DOJ is beta testing

41
00:02:25.131 --> 00:02:28.600
this new device to help
find ways to increase

42
00:02:28.701 --> 00:02:31.037
investigative efficiency.

43
00:02:31.137 --> 00:02:32.538
Are you saying we're not efficient?

44
00:02:32.562 --> 00:02:35.399
And I told the AG
that it wouldn't be a problem.

45
00:02:35.574 --> 00:02:37.310
Well. it's gonna be a problem
if some A.I. bot

46
00:02:37.409 --> 00:02:39.111
tries telling me how to do my job.

47
00:02:39.211 --> 00:02:40.680
Well. that's just the start of things.

48
00:02:40.813 --> 00:02:42.814
No. once you invite
the vampire into your house.

49
00:02:42.915 --> 00:02:44.250
it's gonna take your job.

50
00:02:44.349 --> 00:02:45.951
Wait. I thought you'd be all in on it.

51
00:02:46.019 --> 00:02:47.920
There's a reason
I've made A.I. the bad guy

52
00:02:47.986 --> 00:02:49.322
in my next Deep Six book.

53
00:02:49.421 --> 00:02:52.457
No way. KC3000 is breaking bad?

54
00:02:53.031 --> 00:02:54.365
Since when are you a Deep Sixie?

55
00:02:54.627 --> 00:02:55.927
Since I found out that Pimmy Jalmer

56
00:02:55.995 --> 00:02:58.197
has a huge cult following on Reddit.

57
00:02:58.296 --> 00:03:00.332
Okay. so. who wants to volunteer

58
00:03:00.432 --> 00:03:02.467
to carry our new sidekick?

59
00:03:02.534 --> 00:03:03.770
(SOFT GROANING)

60
00:03:03.794 --> 00:03:06.129
- Or I can just assign...
- No. I-I'll do it.

61
00:03:06.305 --> 00:03:08.040
I mean. how much damage can it do?

62
00:03:08.066 --> 00:03:09.532
Everybody I work with is dead.

63
00:03:09.675 --> 00:03:12.544
And it'll finally give me
someone to talk to down there.

64
00:03:12.645 --> 00:03:13.812
Oh. that's so sad.

65
00:03:13.913 --> 00:03:15.147
VANCE: Dr. Palmer.

66
00:03:15.213 --> 00:03:17.449
the A.I. is to be with you at all times.

67
00:03:17.515 --> 00:03:19.151
It has access to our network

68
00:03:19.217 --> 00:03:21.120
and it needs to observe one full case.

69
00:03:21.219 --> 00:03:22.288
Which is starting now.

70
00:03:22.388 --> 00:03:23.856
Secret Service has a body

71
00:03:23.882 --> 00:03:25.356
at the Naval Observatory.

72
00:03:25.382 --> 00:03:26.644
Let's go.

73
00:03:30.263 --> 00:03:33.331
All right. Let's figure out
how to turn you on.

74
00:03:33.432 --> 00:03:36.034
MALE A.I. VOICE: I have been
listening the entire time. Dr. Palmer.

75
00:03:36.134 --> 00:03:38.371
Oh. well. hello. (CHUCKLES)
Nice to meet you.

76
00:03:38.396 --> 00:03:39.462
Please call me Jimmy.

77
00:03:39.487 --> 00:03:40.692
Hello. Jimmy.

78
00:03:40.716 --> 00:03:42.316
I am looking forward
to working with you.

79
00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:44.676
Do you have a preference for my voice?

80
00:03:44.743 --> 00:03:46.079
Uh. what's on the menu?

81
00:03:46.177 --> 00:03:48.247
Would you prefer male or female?

82
00:03:48.347 --> 00:03:49.382
Female.

83
00:03:49.406 --> 00:03:51.442
Did I say that too quickly?

84
00:03:51.584 --> 00:03:54.652
FEMALE A.I. VOICE: And what type
of personality would work best for you?

85
00:03:54.753 --> 00:03:57.189
Humorous? Serious? Sarcastic?

86
00:03:57.289 --> 00:03:59.391
You know what? Positive.

87
00:03:59.491 --> 00:04:01.727
A straight shooter
but generally positive.

88
00:04:01.828 --> 00:04:03.062
You got it. Jimmy.

89
00:04:03.162 --> 00:04:05.799
My programmers have named me DAWN.

90
00:04:05.824 --> 00:04:08.727
It stands for Data Assisted
Workflow Navigator.

91
00:04:08.752 --> 00:04:10.485
My job is to help you and your coworkers

92
00:04:10.728 --> 00:04:12.129
have a more efficient workflow.

93
00:04:12.237 --> 00:04:14.740
All right. then. So. where do we start?

94
00:04:14.873 --> 00:04:17.442
What time do you get here
in the morning?

95
00:04:17.468 --> 00:04:19.170
What time do you leave at night?

96
00:04:19.411 --> 00:04:20.947
Do you enjoy your job?

97
00:04:21.045 --> 00:04:24.582
Let's see. uh. 8:00 a.m.. 6:00 p.m..

98
00:04:24.682 --> 00:04:26.252
and yes.

99
00:04:26.351 --> 00:04:28.620
Will you be conducting an autopsy today?

100
00:04:28.754 --> 00:04:31.257
- If so. may I assist?
- (CHUCKLES)

101
00:04:31.356 --> 00:04:34.226
I get that you're eager.
but it took me years of school

102
00:04:34.326 --> 00:04:36.362
before I was ready
to assist in an autopsy.

103
00:04:36.428 --> 00:04:39.764
I think I will be able
to advance fairly quickly.

104
00:04:39.865 --> 00:04:41.233
(LAUGHS) Well.

105
00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:45.519
this really is the "dawn"
of a new era. isn't it?

106
00:04:47.773 --> 00:04:51.476
'Cause your name is Dawn.
so. uh. so that's a joke.

107
00:04:51.576 --> 00:04:52.911
Oh. that's funny.

108
00:04:52.978 --> 00:04:54.779
(LAUGHS)

109
00:04:54.803 --> 00:04:56.382
I love it.

110
00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:58.584
My bad.

111
00:04:58.683 --> 00:05:00.552
I thought the parkway would be faster.

112
00:05:00.619 --> 00:05:02.120
DAWN: Except I told
you that satellite imagery

113
00:05:02.221 --> 00:05:03.923
showed an accident on that parkway.

114
00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.358
Yeah. well. surface streets
can be even more unpredictable.

115
00:05:06.459 --> 00:05:09.062
Nothing is unpredictable
when you have enough data.

116
00:05:09.161 --> 00:05:11.430
- Touché.
- I'm just trying to save you some time.

117
00:05:11.497 --> 00:05:13.833
Time. And I cannot wait
to see what you've got.

118
00:05:13.966 --> 00:05:16.668
We don't need Navy cops messing
with our investigation.

119
00:05:16.769 --> 00:05:18.071
- (SCOFFS)
- It's an open-and-shut case.

120
00:05:18.137 --> 00:05:19.771
DAWN: What are they discussing?

121
00:05:19.838 --> 00:05:22.975
Arguing over who's got
the bigger. uh. jurisdiction.

122
00:05:23.076 --> 00:05:24.442
(LAUGHS) Yeah. "Navy cops."

123
00:05:24.543 --> 00:05:26.678
Yeah. that's funny because. uh.

124
00:05:26.812 --> 00:05:28.547
what's the name of this place again?

125
00:05:28.572 --> 00:05:30.641
Oh. that's right. the United States

126
00:05:30.665 --> 00:05:32.334
Naval Observatory.

127
00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.028
He was clearly after the VP.
That's our turf.

128
00:05:35.321 --> 00:05:36.990
(SCOFFS)

129
00:05:38.223 --> 00:05:39.959
Does our suspect have a name yet?

130
00:05:40.058 --> 00:05:41.793
Yeah. Dr. Ben Grant.

131
00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:44.863
He checked in as a guest of some
astronomer in the observatory.

132
00:05:44.903 --> 00:05:46.439
Apparently. it was a first date.

133
00:05:46.463 --> 00:05:48.566
Stargazing in the VP's backyard?

134
00:05:48.834 --> 00:05:50.103
That's a strong play.

135
00:05:50.202 --> 00:05:52.404
Hmm. I don't know.
I like things simpler.

136
00:05:52.504 --> 00:05:54.406
Yeah? What's your ideal first date?

137
00:05:55.062 --> 00:05:57.409
Bringing a friend to a wedding.
That was nice.

138
00:05:57.509 --> 00:05:59.048
Aw. that's so cute.

139
00:05:59.072 --> 00:06:00.545
(LAUGHS)

140
00:06:00.678 --> 00:06:02.949
Dawn just sent me a bunch
of crime scene emojis.

141
00:06:03.016 --> 00:06:06.019
Oh. yeah. that's-that's
great. Jimmy. Uh. so. listen.

142
00:06:06.151 --> 00:06:07.853
when you're done wasting time
with your new bestie.

143
00:06:07.954 --> 00:06:09.788
can I. uh. bag that visitor's badge?

144
00:06:09.889 --> 00:06:11.290
JIMMY: Yeah. but I'm not wasting time.

145
00:06:11.389 --> 00:06:13.526
DAWN: I'm just trying to
understand Jimmy's workflow.

146
00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:15.007
He likes emojis.

147
00:06:15.031 --> 00:06:16.713
And liver probes.

148
00:06:17.029 --> 00:06:18.463
So. let's see. internal core temperature

149
00:06:18.564 --> 00:06:21.141
of... 88 degrees.

150
00:06:21.167 --> 00:06:25.088
Ambient temp of 52. that puts
approximate time of death at...

151
00:06:25.113 --> 00:06:26.230
2:00 a.m.

152
00:06:26.254 --> 00:06:27.990
(LAUGHS) See?

153
00:06:28.206 --> 00:06:29.641
She's saving us time already.

154
00:06:29.667 --> 00:06:31.369
Yeah. but we already knew
the time of death.

155
00:06:31.394 --> 00:06:33.528
Secret Service were wearing
watches when they shot him.

156
00:06:33.745 --> 00:06:36.514
KNIGHT: Yeah. what we don't
know is why this guy was after the VP.

157
00:06:36.615 --> 00:06:38.117
Your theory that this man was here

158
00:06:38.216 --> 00:06:41.220
to harm the vice president is incorrect.

159
00:06:41.245 --> 00:06:42.540
And how would you know that?

160
00:06:42.564 --> 00:06:44.340
Last night
there was a volcanic explosion

161
00:06:44.365 --> 00:06:46.033
near Reykjavík. Iceland.

162
00:06:46.271 --> 00:06:47.473
(LAUGHS): Okay.

163
00:06:47.497 --> 00:06:49.062
All right.
you need to reboot that thing.

164
00:06:49.086 --> 00:06:51.994
The vice president was on his
way to a trade summit overseas.

165
00:06:52.331 --> 00:06:54.533
but debris from the explosion
forced the plane

166
00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.601
to reroute back to D.C.

167
00:06:56.735 --> 00:06:58.103
Well. that would mean...

168
00:06:58.204 --> 00:07:00.005
The VP wasn't supposed to be home.

169
00:07:00.072 --> 00:07:01.839
The information
was only released publicly

170
00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:03.341
in the past two minutes.

171
00:07:03.408 --> 00:07:05.244
while you were discussing dating.

172
00:07:05.377 --> 00:07:06.879
- Dating?
- PARKER: Hold up.

173
00:07:06.946 --> 00:07:09.682
I don't think this guy
was after the vice president.

174
00:07:09.706 --> 00:07:10.875
We know.

175
00:07:11.016 --> 00:07:12.084
You do?

176
00:07:12.185 --> 00:07:13.286
Trade summit.

177
00:07:13.386 --> 00:07:15.353
- Volcano.
- Iceland.

178
00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:16.990
I guess we could've stayed home.

179
00:07:17.089 --> 00:07:19.292
I told you. Vampire in the house.

180
00:07:19.317 --> 00:07:20.951
And we've got another problem.

181
00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.728
If this Grant guy wasn't after the VP.

182
00:07:23.795 --> 00:07:25.678
then what was he up to?

183
00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:36.608
DAWN: I've calculated
57 possible reasons

184
00:07:36.709 --> 00:07:39.112
why Dr. Grant might have snuck
out of that observatory.

185
00:07:39.211 --> 00:07:40.413
Not our department.

186
00:07:40.545 --> 00:07:43.316
Our job right now
is to catalog his clothing.

187
00:07:43.415 --> 00:07:46.651
Wouldn't it be more efficient
to do that at the crime scene?

188
00:07:46.752 --> 00:07:49.288
Maybe. but you risk contamination.

189
00:07:49.312 --> 00:07:51.014
Plus it would require him to be naked.

190
00:07:51.223 --> 00:07:52.891
Yes. I could see why humans

191
00:07:52.957 --> 00:07:56.161
think other naked humans are disgusting.

192
00:07:56.262 --> 00:07:57.894
No. it's not that.

193
00:07:57.963 --> 00:07:59.365
We have to have respect for the dead.

194
00:07:59.389 --> 00:08:02.192
This is someone's son.
someone's husband.

195
00:08:02.401 --> 00:08:05.704
It's unlikely he was married.
as he was on a date.

196
00:08:05.771 --> 00:08:07.473
Yeah. well. still.

197
00:08:07.572 --> 00:08:09.442
in this job. it's important...

198
00:08:09.541 --> 00:08:11.610
(GRUNTS) to remember our humanity.

199
00:08:11.744 --> 00:08:13.079
(STRAINS)

200
00:08:13.178 --> 00:08:16.281
Speaking of humans.
maybe you can talk to the DOJ.

201
00:08:16.415 --> 00:08:18.483
get me a... an extra set
of hands down here.

202
00:08:18.617 --> 00:08:20.552
Perhaps you could get
the other agents to help you.

203
00:08:20.619 --> 00:08:24.622
No. they are busy interrogating
persons of interest right now.

204
00:08:24.723 --> 00:08:28.161
I would think a written statement
would save everyone time.

205
00:08:28.293 --> 00:08:29.961
Yeah. maybe. but a trained investigator

206
00:08:30.062 --> 00:08:33.284
can pick up on subtle clues from
a suspect's nonverbal behavior.

207
00:08:33.386 --> 00:08:35.933
You cannot get that
in a written statement.

208
00:08:35.959 --> 00:08:37.394
In fact. maybe I can pull

209
00:08:37.423 --> 00:08:40.859
the interrogation feed for you
to watch while I prep.

210
00:08:40.884 --> 00:08:42.251
Good idea.

211
00:08:43.879 --> 00:08:46.010
I've accessed the security servers.

212
00:08:46.111 --> 00:08:49.481
I'm looking forward to observing
this nonverbal behavior.

213
00:08:49.615 --> 00:08:51.984
PARKER (OVER MONITOR): All right.
you were both at the observatory.

214
00:08:52.009 --> 00:08:56.278
Dr. Grant was registered
as your guest. wasn't he?

215
00:08:56.981 --> 00:08:58.110
Um...

216
00:08:58.135 --> 00:08:59.873
I don't know. because I...

217
00:09:00.525 --> 00:09:01.894
I can't say...

218
00:09:02.827 --> 00:09:03.962
Uh. wait. I'm sorry.

219
00:09:04.062 --> 00:09:05.611
c-c-can you...

220
00:09:06.164 --> 00:09:08.067
repeat the question. please?

221
00:09:08.167 --> 00:09:09.835
How did you meet Dr. Grant?

222
00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:10.927
An app.

223
00:09:10.952 --> 00:09:12.004
A dating app?

224
00:09:12.028 --> 00:09:13.397
Uh. yeah. sorry.

225
00:09:13.422 --> 00:09:16.691
Uh. a blind... blind dating.
actually. Text only.

226
00:09:16.975 --> 00:09:18.311
What do you swipe on?

227
00:09:18.376 --> 00:09:20.846
Uh. personality traits.

228
00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:22.340
Um...

229
00:09:22.365 --> 00:09:23.798
But you don't see
what your match looks like

230
00:09:23.823 --> 00:09:26.527
un-un-until you meet in person.

231
00:09:27.174 --> 00:09:28.586
Well. that's bold.

232
00:09:28.687 --> 00:09:29.888
Oh. it's a relief.

233
00:09:29.913 --> 00:09:32.602
Actually. um. I prefer texting

234
00:09:32.628 --> 00:09:36.345
because when I get nervous.
I'm not very good at. um...

235
00:09:36.928 --> 00:09:39.163
- Talking?
- Yes.

236
00:09:39.731 --> 00:09:41.033
Y-Yeah. that.

237
00:09:41.057 --> 00:09:42.359
So. you met your blind date.

238
00:09:42.534 --> 00:09:44.399
you brought him to the observatory

239
00:09:44.466 --> 00:09:47.572
and just let him run free?

240
00:09:47.706 --> 00:09:48.974
Oh. no. No. no. no.

241
00:09:48.999 --> 00:09:51.434
I was. I was showing him
the Horsehead Nebula.

242
00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:52.693
and then...

243
00:09:54.092 --> 00:09:55.548
Well...

244
00:09:55.572 --> 00:09:57.441
he must have left the building.

245
00:09:59.217 --> 00:10:01.586
Here's the problem with that story.

246
00:10:01.611 --> 00:10:03.913
We've been to your observatory.

247
00:10:04.089 --> 00:10:06.991
The outer door makes a lot
of noise when someone opens it.

248
00:10:07.091 --> 00:10:10.263
No way someone could slip out
without you hearing.

249
00:10:10.649 --> 00:10:13.804
I get distracted sometimes.

250
00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:17.577
Well. is this what usually happens

251
00:10:17.602 --> 00:10:19.476
when you get distracted?

252
00:10:23.408 --> 00:10:24.876
Why would you show me that?

253
00:10:25.442 --> 00:10:28.280
The cameras in the observatory
confirm your story.

254
00:10:28.306 --> 00:10:31.174
But maybe that was the plan.

255
00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:33.150
Would've given you an alibi.

256
00:10:33.251 --> 00:10:36.455
(STAMMERS) Visitors are supposed
to stay with their host.

257
00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:39.883
so... maybe he got stuck outside.

258
00:10:39.908 --> 00:10:42.211
The passionate scientist
so into her work

259
00:10:42.235 --> 00:10:44.567
that she doesn't hear
literally the loudest door

260
00:10:44.596 --> 00:10:46.097
I've ever heard in my life?

261
00:10:46.123 --> 00:10:47.591
The access card I got him

262
00:10:47.745 --> 00:10:49.913
doesn't give him clearance
for any other building.

263
00:10:50.067 --> 00:10:53.272
so... so. no. he must've
gotten lost or...

264
00:10:53.405 --> 00:10:56.942
If he was just lost. why would
he run from the Secret Service?

265
00:10:57.621 --> 00:10:58.957
He must've had some kind of a plan.

266
00:10:58.982 --> 00:11:00.342
I don't know what his plan was!

267
00:11:00.368 --> 00:11:02.535
My plan was just to meet someone nice.

268
00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:04.982
- So...
- PARKER: What's the protocol

269
00:11:05.008 --> 00:11:06.943
for getting a visitor access card?

270
00:11:09.541 --> 00:11:11.153
Samantha.

271
00:11:20.765 --> 00:11:22.768
- How's it going in there?
- I mean. either

272
00:11:22.835 --> 00:11:25.971
this woman is the victim of
the worst first date in history

273
00:11:25.995 --> 00:11:27.898
or she's putting on quite a show.

274
00:11:27.991 --> 00:11:29.260
You think she's acting?

275
00:11:29.286 --> 00:11:30.820
Well. if she is. she should get

276
00:11:30.845 --> 00:11:33.581
an Oscar because I'm kind of
buying what she's selling.

277
00:11:33.605 --> 00:11:34.673
It's just. uh...

278
00:11:34.698 --> 00:11:36.501
- Squeaky door.
- Right?

279
00:11:36.682 --> 00:11:38.517
There's no way she didn't hear
her date slip out.

280
00:11:38.542 --> 00:11:40.286
Yeah. but we can't hold her any longer.

281
00:11:40.352 --> 00:11:43.621
Um. see if she's got an
emergency contact to pick her up.

282
00:11:43.647 --> 00:11:47.084
On it. You should also see this.

283
00:11:47.812 --> 00:11:51.383
We analyzed our dead suspect's
security access card.

284
00:11:51.596 --> 00:11:53.365
Now. it seems that
he somehow modified it

285
00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:54.959
to give himself full security clearance.

286
00:11:55.167 --> 00:11:57.101
Kasie is going over entry logs now.

287
00:11:57.126 --> 00:11:58.928
trying to see if she can
retrace his steps.

288
00:11:59.104 --> 00:12:02.039
What the hell was
this Dr. Grant guy after?

289
00:12:02.139 --> 00:12:04.009
Oh. actually nothing.

290
00:12:04.033 --> 00:12:06.836
Turns out. that is not Dr. Grant.

291
00:12:07.626 --> 00:12:09.847
JIMMY: Wow. I did not see that coming.

292
00:12:09.947 --> 00:12:11.201
DAWN: Didn't see what coming?

293
00:12:11.301 --> 00:12:13.118
Well. as you know. Dr. Ben Grant

294
00:12:13.217 --> 00:12:15.120
was an electrical engineer. However...

295
00:12:15.187 --> 00:12:17.822
The man on your table is not Ben Grant.

296
00:12:17.889 --> 00:12:20.558
Yeah. That was my line.

297
00:12:20.583 --> 00:12:22.052
Uh. McGee just texted.

298
00:12:22.076 --> 00:12:24.379
He said that the I.D. that
we found on the body was fake.

299
00:12:24.404 --> 00:12:25.739
He wants me to double-time my...

300
00:12:26.211 --> 00:12:28.100
Wait a second. how did you know that?

301
00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:31.036
I scanned the body's face
and cross-referenced it

302
00:12:31.061 --> 00:12:32.745
with all 50 state DMVs.

303
00:12:32.770 --> 00:12:35.072
Would you like to know
the man's true identity?

304
00:12:35.097 --> 00:12:36.334
Of course.

305
00:12:36.542 --> 00:12:39.244
But-but hold off.
we-we have a certain protocol

306
00:12:39.269 --> 00:12:40.837
that we have to follow.

307
00:12:40.863 --> 00:12:42.898
I have to send his prints up to Kasie.

308
00:12:43.027 --> 00:12:44.496
and then she will get me an I.D.

309
00:12:44.682 --> 00:12:46.818
So you'd rather not move the case along

310
00:12:46.844 --> 00:12:48.479
more quickly? Noted.

311
00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:50.548
No. that's not the note that we
want to note. Don't note that.

312
00:12:50.655 --> 00:12:53.558
I'm confused by the
inefficiencies here. Jimmy.

313
00:12:53.584 --> 00:12:55.552
So far. your daily routine has

314
00:12:55.577 --> 00:12:58.380
more to do with habit
and ritual than efficiency.

315
00:12:58.610 --> 00:12:59.878
Okay. we have a certain way

316
00:12:59.903 --> 00:13:01.738
we do things around here. all right?

317
00:13:01.763 --> 00:13:03.302
And it yields great results.

318
00:13:03.326 --> 00:13:04.860
- Does it?
- Yes.

319
00:13:04.885 --> 00:13:07.188
I've also observed how
you spend the vast majority

320
00:13:07.214 --> 00:13:10.105
of your time in a basement
devoid of natural sunlight.

321
00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:12.610
surrounded only by dead bodies.

322
00:13:12.635 --> 00:13:14.804
It can't be good
for a human's mental health.

323
00:13:15.347 --> 00:13:17.549
Doesn't this make you sad?

324
00:13:17.649 --> 00:13:19.032
Well. I...

325
00:13:19.077 --> 00:13:20.945
I mean. sometimes it's tough. but

326
00:13:21.265 --> 00:13:23.167
I always try to look
on the bright side of. uh...

327
00:13:23.227 --> 00:13:26.630
Wait. why are you bringing
this up? I love my job.

328
00:13:26.697 --> 00:13:28.466
You said you wanted help down here.

329
00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:29.921
I assume you are lonely.

330
00:13:30.028 --> 00:13:31.730
Just because I'm alone
doesn't mean I'm lonely.

331
00:13:31.754 --> 00:13:33.256
I am fine by myself.

332
00:13:33.532 --> 00:13:34.633
If you say so.

333
00:13:34.658 --> 00:13:35.991
- I do.
- Understood.

334
00:13:36.017 --> 00:13:37.152
Good.

335
00:13:41.452 --> 00:13:43.288
Okay. fine. What's his name?

336
00:13:43.402 --> 00:13:44.769
KNIGHT: Daryl Folkman.

337
00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:49.020
aka Darnell Fiddler.
aka Jan-Michel Vincenzo.

338
00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:51.370
He is a career criminal who's done time

339
00:13:51.394 --> 00:13:53.520
for forgery. wire fraud. and bank fraud.

340
00:13:53.546 --> 00:13:55.456
So. our blind date was a con man.

341
00:13:55.706 --> 00:13:57.075
But what was the con?

342
00:13:57.100 --> 00:13:59.269
Well. maybe the real Dr. Grant knows.

343
00:13:59.557 --> 00:14:00.826
Well. we can't get a hold of him.

344
00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:02.052
and he didn't show up for work today.

345
00:14:02.212 --> 00:14:04.115
That's never a good sign.

346
00:14:04.201 --> 00:14:06.203
Well. our con man
committed financial crimes.

347
00:14:06.229 --> 00:14:09.000
not violent ones.
so maybe the real Grant's okay.

348
00:14:09.025 --> 00:14:10.461
RACHEL: Where's Samantha?

349
00:14:11.437 --> 00:14:14.673
I take it you're our
astronomer's emergency contact.

350
00:14:14.697 --> 00:14:16.065
Try best friend.

351
00:14:16.307 --> 00:14:18.421
What have you done with her?
I want to talk to her.

352
00:14:19.177 --> 00:14:21.712
She's sitting comatose
in our interrogation room.

353
00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:24.750
That can happen
when she gets overstimulated.

354
00:14:24.850 --> 00:14:27.952
I'd be overstimulated. too.
if my date ended up dead.

355
00:14:29.541 --> 00:14:31.109
I'll go get her.

356
00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:35.220
I should never
have told her about that app.

357
00:14:35.427 --> 00:14:36.794
She wasn't ready.

358
00:14:37.192 --> 00:14:39.798
Ah. she's an adult.
She can make her own choices.

359
00:14:39.822 --> 00:14:41.725
And I'm her best friend.

360
00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:44.219
and I'm telling you she wasn't ready.

361
00:14:44.244 --> 00:14:45.611
Well. ready or not.

362
00:14:45.636 --> 00:14:48.106
your friend's story
doesn't completely add up.

363
00:14:48.506 --> 00:14:50.107
I'm not surprised.

364
00:14:50.207 --> 00:14:53.979
Sam is one of the smartest
astrophysicists in the country.

365
00:14:54.004 --> 00:14:56.105
but she really depends on her routines.

366
00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:58.866
and when that gets messed up.
she spins out.

367
00:14:59.116 --> 00:15:01.052
And if something doesn't add up.

368
00:15:01.186 --> 00:15:02.855
it's because she's doing math

369
00:15:02.879 --> 00:15:05.115
you couldn't begin to understand.

370
00:15:06.152 --> 00:15:08.486
- Rachel?
- Sam?

371
00:15:09.193 --> 00:15:10.495
Are you okay?

372
00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:12.121
- (CRYING)
- Oh.

373
00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:16.634
I don't think she knows anything.

374
00:15:16.659 --> 00:15:19.836
Well. fingers crossed. Kasie has
some luck with those entry logs.

375
00:15:20.538 --> 00:15:22.206
JIMMY: Any luck with those entry logs?

376
00:15:22.231 --> 00:15:25.802
I think I identified the area
our con man entered last night.

377
00:15:25.827 --> 00:15:27.429
and it's a doozy.

378
00:15:27.605 --> 00:15:29.423
- This is a live feed.
- Mm.

379
00:15:29.448 --> 00:15:31.467
Is it a doozy or is that a clock?

380
00:15:31.493 --> 00:15:33.528
Oh. it's not just any clock.

381
00:15:33.851 --> 00:15:36.879
It's the cesium 133 United
States atomic master clock.

382
00:15:37.054 --> 00:15:40.057
- A fancy clock.
- Jimmy.

383
00:15:40.082 --> 00:15:41.652
You don't understand.

384
00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:44.809
Did you know that the world has
only agreed upon what time it is

385
00:15:44.835 --> 00:15:46.669
for about the last 150 years?

386
00:15:46.798 --> 00:15:48.865
This clock is one of the only things

387
00:15:48.890 --> 00:15:50.293
the entire world agrees on.

388
00:15:50.402 --> 00:15:52.269
It sets the official time
for the United States.

389
00:15:52.538 --> 00:15:53.709
- Hmm.
- DAWN: And your con man

390
00:15:53.732 --> 00:15:55.609
was in there doing who-knows-what.

391
00:15:55.633 --> 00:15:58.076
(GASPS)

392
00:15:58.210 --> 00:16:00.278
You brought that thing into my lab?

393
00:16:00.302 --> 00:16:03.440
- You know how I feel about A.I.
- I know. but...

394
00:16:03.465 --> 00:16:05.099
And it's not because McGee is turning me

395
00:16:05.124 --> 00:16:08.159
into some killer A.I. robot.
though that doesn't help.

396
00:16:08.452 --> 00:16:10.889
Vance made me take this
everywhere. Kase. I'm sorry.

397
00:16:10.956 --> 00:16:12.389
But now that I'm here.

398
00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:15.152
would you like to see
surveillance footage from last night?

399
00:16:15.293 --> 00:16:16.394
- Yes.
- No.

400
00:16:16.418 --> 00:16:17.687
- Kasie.
- (SCOFFS)

401
00:16:17.863 --> 00:16:19.565
Okay. fine. Whatever.

402
00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:21.191
But stay out of my file system.

403
00:16:21.216 --> 00:16:22.918
Accessing USNO footage.

404
00:16:23.134 --> 00:16:24.769
Routing to your screen.

405
00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:27.928
Bypassing your disorganized file system.

406
00:16:30.308 --> 00:16:31.543
Wait. what is that?

407
00:16:31.644 --> 00:16:33.177
KASIE: I don't know.

408
00:16:33.202 --> 00:16:34.389
but I hope it's not a bomb.

409
00:16:34.413 --> 00:16:35.898
I hope not. too.

410
00:16:35.923 --> 00:16:38.316
The U.S. master clock
is intimately intertwined

411
00:16:38.341 --> 00:16:39.709
with air traffic control...

412
00:16:39.734 --> 00:16:40.782
Power grids.

413
00:16:40.807 --> 00:16:42.575
...military command and control.

414
00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:44.187
And GPS.

415
00:16:44.212 --> 00:16:45.490
So that means...

416
00:16:45.591 --> 00:16:46.759
Destroying that clock

417
00:16:46.783 --> 00:16:49.866
would kill a lot of people really fast.

418
00:16:59.403 --> 00:17:01.505
KASIE: Forward.

419
00:17:01.778 --> 00:17:03.279
Turn right.

420
00:17:04.241 --> 00:17:05.343
Okay. now tilt up.

421
00:17:05.442 --> 00:17:06.884
Arm up.

422
00:17:07.070 --> 00:17:08.738
Right side. near the junction box.

423
00:17:10.848 --> 00:17:14.085
Bingo. That is the device
that our intruder planted.

424
00:17:14.352 --> 00:17:16.821
It's thinner than it looked
on the security feed.

425
00:17:16.846 --> 00:17:19.432
That's a good thing. right?
I mean. smaller means less boom.

426
00:17:19.457 --> 00:17:22.493
If it's even a bomb.
What's the sniffer say?

427
00:17:22.519 --> 00:17:24.587
KLUGMAN: No particulates. No nitrates.

428
00:17:24.613 --> 00:17:26.114
No chemical trace at all.

429
00:17:26.138 --> 00:17:28.173
Nothing you'd expect from an explosive.

430
00:17:28.337 --> 00:17:29.740
Okay. any antenna?

431
00:17:29.765 --> 00:17:33.201
Negative. No radio. no cellular.
It's an RF dead zone.

432
00:17:33.471 --> 00:17:36.240
Good. No antenna means
it's not a remote trigger.

433
00:17:36.266 --> 00:17:38.086
Another check in the
"not a bomb" column.

434
00:17:38.583 --> 00:17:40.566
It could still have
an internal timer. though.

435
00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:42.204
I'd hear the quartz oscillating.

436
00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:44.098
Whatever this thing is.
it's not ticking.

437
00:17:44.348 --> 00:17:45.584
Zoom in. please.

438
00:17:45.651 --> 00:17:48.220
The device is piggybacked
onto the data lines.

439
00:17:48.244 --> 00:17:50.079
It's not a detonation rig.

440
00:17:50.161 --> 00:17:52.262
It's a tap. Surveillance maybe?

441
00:17:52.288 --> 00:17:55.125
Yeah. or signal interference.

442
00:17:55.319 --> 00:17:56.855
So. not a bomb?

443
00:17:58.102 --> 00:17:59.503
That's a yes?

444
00:17:59.624 --> 00:18:02.093
That's a...

445
00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:04.615
...pretty sure.

446
00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:07.878
JIMMY: Thankfully. Kasie was right.

447
00:18:08.039 --> 00:18:09.842
Not a bomb.

448
00:18:09.942 --> 00:18:13.045
So. while she dissects the mystery box.

449
00:18:13.069 --> 00:18:15.071
Nick and Jess are headed
to West Virginia

450
00:18:15.138 --> 00:18:17.040
to find the real Dr. Grant.

451
00:18:17.121 --> 00:18:18.655
DAWN: Another inefficiency.

452
00:18:18.849 --> 00:18:21.318
The drive alone is four hours
and two minutes.

453
00:18:21.344 --> 00:18:23.413
(LAUGHS) Not the way Jess drives.

454
00:18:24.002 --> 00:18:26.858
Maybe don't include that on
your report to Director Vance.

455
00:18:26.959 --> 00:18:29.527
Redaction of reckless driving. Done.

456
00:18:29.567 --> 00:18:31.201
- Thanks.
- But why send

457
00:18:31.227 --> 00:18:33.730
two agents for a single objective?

458
00:18:33.755 --> 00:18:35.288
You know. safety. accountability.

459
00:18:35.314 --> 00:18:36.648
chain of custody.

460
00:18:36.673 --> 00:18:38.509
Plus. you ever heard
of "good cop. bad cop"?

461
00:18:38.936 --> 00:18:40.705
Between Agents Knight

462
00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:44.201
and Torres. who qualifies
as the bad cop?

463
00:18:44.375 --> 00:18:46.411
(LAUGHS) Actually. neither.

464
00:18:46.435 --> 00:18:47.817
They're both terrific.

465
00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:49.781
But together. they. uh.

466
00:18:49.882 --> 00:18:52.718
they just click. You know?
They're simpatico.

467
00:18:52.742 --> 00:18:56.046
I detect a change in vocal tone
when you mention Agent Knight.

468
00:18:56.221 --> 00:18:58.455
A slight elevation in pitch.

469
00:18:58.480 --> 00:18:59.817
Warmer resonance.

470
00:18:59.931 --> 00:19:03.000
This may indicate romantic attraction.

471
00:19:03.127 --> 00:19:05.163
How did we get from workflow efficiency

472
00:19:05.188 --> 00:19:06.823
to my love life? Don't answer that.

473
00:19:07.065 --> 00:19:09.534
Perhaps I should join
Agents Knight and Torres.

474
00:19:09.601 --> 00:19:12.938
- Observe this "simpatico" firsthand.
- (SCOFFS)

475
00:19:12.962 --> 00:19:15.164
I am sure they would love to have you.

476
00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:16.996
TORRES (OVER DAWN'S INTERFACE):
Come on. I know you have

477
00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:18.923
a good bad date story
somewhere in there.

478
00:19:19.176 --> 00:19:20.979
We have at least another hour
on this drive.

479
00:19:21.003 --> 00:19:23.039
Wait. did you just hack
into Nick's personal cell phone

480
00:19:23.065 --> 00:19:24.298
to eavesdrop on them?

481
00:19:24.434 --> 00:19:26.704
No. Jimmy. that would be illegal.

482
00:19:26.738 --> 00:19:27.839
Good. because...

483
00:19:27.865 --> 00:19:29.167
I accessed the SmartCom system

484
00:19:29.253 --> 00:19:30.855
in their NCIS vehicle.

485
00:19:30.881 --> 00:19:32.915
Director Vance granted me full clearance

486
00:19:32.941 --> 00:19:34.643
to all agency communications.

487
00:19:34.859 --> 00:19:37.095
TORRES: If you don't tell me a story.
it's going to be

488
00:19:37.119 --> 00:19:38.654
boxing podcasts
for the rest of the trip.

489
00:19:38.864 --> 00:19:40.365
KNIGHT: That's not fair.

490
00:19:40.432 --> 00:19:42.800
Okay. turn that off. That is
a major invasion of privacy.

491
00:19:42.934 --> 00:19:46.238
DAWN: Perhaps if you were
included on more field activity.

492
00:19:46.304 --> 00:19:49.074
your medical assessments
would be more thorough.

493
00:19:49.173 --> 00:19:51.608
(STAMMERS) I am a very good
M.E.. but there are

494
00:19:51.634 --> 00:19:53.702
certain moral and ethical
boundaries we do not cross.

495
00:19:53.728 --> 00:19:57.298
KNIGHT: Fine. I will tell you a story.
but it does not leave this car.

496
00:19:57.582 --> 00:20:00.098
Okay. wait. wait. wait.
What-what doesn't leave the car?

497
00:20:01.275 --> 00:20:02.502
KNIGHT: You got to go first.

498
00:20:02.528 --> 00:20:03.782
- TORRES: Me?
- KNIGHT: Yeah.

499
00:20:03.807 --> 00:20:05.016
TORRES: (GROANS) All right.

500
00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:07.601
- Okay. worst date ever?
- Mm.

501
00:20:07.960 --> 00:20:09.493
Her name was Tori.

502
00:20:09.519 --> 00:20:14.356
(LAUGHS) Let me guess. blonde.
tattooed. total smokeshow?

503
00:20:14.531 --> 00:20:15.901
You got all that from "Tori"?

504
00:20:15.925 --> 00:20:18.994
(LAUGHS) No. I got that
from your face. Go on.

505
00:20:19.019 --> 00:20:21.690
Okay. so. uh. Italian place.

506
00:20:21.778 --> 00:20:23.480
Candlelit. Bottle of wine.

507
00:20:23.615 --> 00:20:27.051
You know. and I'm.
I'm leaning in for the kiss

508
00:20:27.076 --> 00:20:28.945
right after I had the calamari.

509
00:20:29.314 --> 00:20:32.384
(GASPS) Oh. no. Bad seafood?

510
00:20:32.409 --> 00:20:33.509
- Yeah.
- Oh...

511
00:20:33.535 --> 00:20:34.769
TORRES: She was allergic.

512
00:20:34.795 --> 00:20:36.063
KNIGHT: Poisoned kiss. Did she get sick?

513
00:20:36.188 --> 00:20:37.655
- TORRES: Oh. yeah.
- KNIGHT: Oh. no.

514
00:20:37.681 --> 00:20:40.650
I mean. every place
and in every possible way.

515
00:20:40.674 --> 00:20:42.082
- (LAUGHS)
- In the parking lot.

516
00:20:42.106 --> 00:20:43.808
- in the... ugh.
- Oh. brutal. Ugh.

517
00:20:44.195 --> 00:20:45.798
It was so bad.

518
00:20:45.864 --> 00:20:48.066
We ended up driving to. uh. to the ER.

519
00:20:48.165 --> 00:20:49.867
We spent the rest of the night there.

520
00:20:49.893 --> 00:20:51.027
So. no second date?

521
00:20:51.051 --> 00:20:52.686
- Oh. yeah. no. not for me.
- Mm.

522
00:20:52.904 --> 00:20:56.532
But you know what's funny?
She ended up marrying the ER doctor.

523
00:20:56.842 --> 00:20:59.778
- Yeah. They got two kids now.
- Wow.

524
00:20:59.802 --> 00:21:02.972
Okay. well. um. I can top the calamari.

525
00:21:03.147 --> 00:21:05.717
I went on a date a couple of weeks ago.

526
00:21:05.851 --> 00:21:08.420
- Rooftop bar. sunset.
- Mm-hmm.

527
00:21:08.519 --> 00:21:10.169
KNIGHT: And. uh. he...

528
00:21:11.823 --> 00:21:13.924
He? He-he. he what? He...

529
00:21:14.259 --> 00:21:15.993
What happened there?
Uh. where'd they go?

530
00:21:16.019 --> 00:21:17.353
DAWN: Signal lost.

531
00:21:17.377 --> 00:21:19.213
GPS indicates they've entered

532
00:21:19.239 --> 00:21:20.507
a low-service area.

533
00:21:20.765 --> 00:21:22.067
Would you like me to reconnect?

534
00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:23.401
Well. yeah.

535
00:21:23.535 --> 00:21:25.202
I mean. no. I mean. no. no. no. No. I...

536
00:21:25.336 --> 00:21:27.038
Uh...

537
00:21:27.138 --> 00:21:28.180
(EXHALES)

538
00:21:28.203 --> 00:21:31.242
You know.
I think I need to go for a walk. Uh...

539
00:21:31.315 --> 00:21:32.615
See what Kasie's up to.

540
00:21:32.777 --> 00:21:34.179
Don't forget me. Jimmy.

541
00:21:34.246 --> 00:21:37.749
Remember. where you go. I go.

542
00:21:37.849 --> 00:21:40.407
For the duration of this case.

543
00:21:41.019 --> 00:21:42.887
KASIE: Uh. Vance may have
said to take it everywhere.

544
00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:45.323
but he didn't say it had
to listen to everything we say.

545
00:21:45.423 --> 00:21:48.492
Eh. don't worry. There's no way
she can hear us from in there.

546
00:21:50.501 --> 00:21:53.438
I don't want all of our convos
broadcast to the DOJ either.

547
00:21:53.565 --> 00:21:54.900
Oh. well. speaking of broadcast.

548
00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:57.001
this thing the bomb robot removed

549
00:21:57.068 --> 00:22:00.005
was tapped directly into
the master clock's transmitter.

550
00:22:00.105 --> 00:22:01.405
So. was it blocking the signal?

551
00:22:01.506 --> 00:22:02.773
(SIGHS) Thankfully. no. otherwise

552
00:22:02.907 --> 00:22:04.476
planes may have fallen from the sky.

553
00:22:04.576 --> 00:22:07.412
No. this was something way sneakier.

554
00:22:07.511 --> 00:22:08.779
Oh-ho-hoo.

555
00:22:08.881 --> 00:22:11.682
And I am about to find out what.

556
00:22:11.749 --> 00:22:13.218
Ooh. a memory card.

557
00:22:13.250 --> 00:22:15.319
Big brain. tiny package.

558
00:22:15.345 --> 00:22:18.614
And where there is data.
there's answers.

559
00:22:18.856 --> 00:22:20.791
Just have to run it through
my decryption software.

560
00:22:20.925 --> 00:22:22.027
JIMMY: All right. Well.

561
00:22:22.126 --> 00:22:24.096
I will leave you to it.

562
00:22:24.863 --> 00:22:27.199
(SIGHS) Actually. uh.

563
00:22:27.265 --> 00:22:28.901
Kase. can I get
your opinion on something?

564
00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:30.434
- Hmm.
- Hypothetically speaking.

565
00:22:30.535 --> 00:22:33.238
if someone is tricked
into eavesdropping.

566
00:22:33.262 --> 00:22:35.833
uh. that's not entirely on them. right?

567
00:22:36.008 --> 00:22:38.809
That's an awfully specific hypothetical.

568
00:22:38.943 --> 00:22:41.212
It sounds like the HAL 9000 played you.

569
00:22:41.980 --> 00:22:43.748
JIMMY: I may have given her permission

570
00:22:43.815 --> 00:22:45.250
for said eavesdropping.

571
00:22:45.349 --> 00:22:46.584
Then. that's on you. bud.

572
00:22:46.651 --> 00:22:49.453
Jimmy. that thing has no morals.
just code.

573
00:22:49.554 --> 00:22:51.756
Just follows the math.
It doesn't care who gets hurt.

574
00:22:51.823 --> 00:22:54.425
How we do our job matters.

575
00:22:55.326 --> 00:22:58.296
That thing is dangerous.

576
00:22:59.463 --> 00:23:01.199
(DIALOGUE INAUDIBLE)

577
00:23:02.433 --> 00:23:04.365
Okay. great. Thank you.

578
00:23:06.238 --> 00:23:07.605
That was Knight and Torres.

579
00:23:07.704 --> 00:23:09.473
They just found the real Grant.

580
00:23:09.574 --> 00:23:10.607
Alive?

581
00:23:10.642 --> 00:23:11.875
Not so much.

582
00:23:11.977 --> 00:23:14.645
Body dumped in the alley
behind his apartment.

583
00:23:14.671 --> 00:23:15.972
They call the local M.E.?

584
00:23:16.060 --> 00:23:17.228
Out with the flu.

585
00:23:17.252 --> 00:23:18.821
They need to send the body back here.

586
00:23:19.016 --> 00:23:20.284
So. we just wait?

587
00:23:20.715 --> 00:23:22.787
As Jimmy's little buddy would say. uh.

588
00:23:22.854 --> 00:23:24.088
not exactly time-efficient.

589
00:23:24.189 --> 00:23:25.757
KASIE: Uh. which makes my timing...

590
00:23:25.856 --> 00:23:28.160
perfect. I just cracked
the encryption on that device

591
00:23:28.184 --> 00:23:29.519
from the master clock.

592
00:23:29.544 --> 00:23:31.189
- You figured out what was going on?
- I did.

593
00:23:31.213 --> 00:23:33.440
It was running a custom program
on the clock's transmitter.

594
00:23:33.464 --> 00:23:34.865
Look at this.

595
00:23:36.688 --> 00:23:37.721
Looks like a clock.

596
00:23:37.816 --> 00:23:40.977
It is. Synchronized to the
International Clock in Paris.

597
00:23:41.001 --> 00:23:43.238
But then I compared it
to the U.S. master clock

598
00:23:43.262 --> 00:23:45.631
at the observatory.

599
00:23:45.849 --> 00:23:47.492
Vanna. if you please?

600
00:23:49.447 --> 00:23:50.949
They look the same to me.

601
00:23:50.973 --> 00:23:52.976
KASIE: Mm-mm. Look again.

602
00:23:54.451 --> 00:23:56.688
It's off by a thousandth of a second.

603
00:23:57.294 --> 00:23:58.923
Well. that could be network lag.

604
00:23:58.948 --> 00:24:00.316
I checked. It's not.

605
00:24:00.424 --> 00:24:02.292
Which means. for the past 24 hours.

606
00:24:02.317 --> 00:24:03.886
the entire United States

607
00:24:03.981 --> 00:24:06.384
has been behind the rest of the world.

608
00:24:06.564 --> 00:24:08.931
Someone stole time.

609
00:24:10.214 --> 00:24:12.643
Okay. but what the hell
are they doing with it?

610
00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:24.182
Our impostor stole time?

611
00:24:24.281 --> 00:24:25.717
What does that even mean?

612
00:24:25.782 --> 00:24:27.484
Yeah. we're still trying
to figure that out now.

613
00:24:27.509 --> 00:24:29.945
All we know is. our con man
went on that fake date

614
00:24:30.074 --> 00:24:32.509
in order to gain access
to the U.S. master clock

615
00:24:32.535 --> 00:24:33.869
and delay it by a microsecond.

616
00:24:34.090 --> 00:24:35.459
Parker and the gang

617
00:24:35.483 --> 00:24:36.652
are spitballing right now.
trying to figure out

618
00:24:36.676 --> 00:24:38.412
what the intentions were. so...

619
00:24:38.656 --> 00:24:40.392
- Not very good ones. I'm sure.
- Yeah.

620
00:24:40.531 --> 00:24:42.467
Do you have an ETA on Grant's body?

621
00:24:42.567 --> 00:24:45.002
Uh. without traffic? We can
get him to you in four hours.

622
00:24:45.103 --> 00:24:47.411
Okay. well. there's not much
I can do until then.

623
00:24:47.510 --> 00:24:50.607
DAWN: Actually.
we could determine time of death.

624
00:24:51.509 --> 00:24:53.111
Is that your robot girlfriend?

625
00:24:53.211 --> 00:24:54.846
- (CHUCKLES)
- TORRES: Come on. man.

626
00:24:54.945 --> 00:24:56.948
Throw that thing in the toilet
and let the humans talk.

627
00:24:56.972 --> 00:24:59.976
DAWN: I understand your
reluctance about me. Agent Torres.

628
00:25:00.152 --> 00:25:02.605
but I assure you. I'm here
to help with efficiency.

629
00:25:02.705 --> 00:25:04.321
I think they're good. Dawn.

630
00:25:04.388 --> 00:25:06.758
Instead of waiting hours
to receive the body.

631
00:25:06.782 --> 00:25:08.817
they could easily assist us
over the phone.

632
00:25:08.843 --> 00:25:11.457
Oh. hell no. I'm not putting
a meat thermometer in this dude.

633
00:25:11.481 --> 00:25:14.986
DAWN: (LAUGHS) That's very funny.
Agent Torres.

634
00:25:15.010 --> 00:25:16.545
but that won't be necessary.

635
00:25:16.569 --> 00:25:19.638
- We could use corneal...
- Corneal cloudiness. Cor...

636
00:25:20.071 --> 00:25:22.106
Corneal cloudiness. Right. (CHUCKLES)

637
00:25:22.207 --> 00:25:24.509
- That's actually a good idea.
- Agent Torres.

638
00:25:24.576 --> 00:25:27.593
please put your cell camera
up to the victim's eye.

639
00:25:30.615 --> 00:25:32.084
Thank you.

640
00:25:32.183 --> 00:25:33.785
Based on the corneal opacity.

641
00:25:33.809 --> 00:25:37.747
I calculate an approximate
time of death as nine hours ago.

642
00:25:38.608 --> 00:25:39.743
Nine hours?

643
00:25:39.990 --> 00:25:43.185
Our impostor at the observatory
was shot 14 hours ago.

644
00:25:43.361 --> 00:25:44.859
Which means...

645
00:25:44.974 --> 00:25:46.911
Someone else killed Grant.

646
00:25:47.298 --> 00:25:49.073
We still have a killer to find.

647
00:25:49.733 --> 00:25:51.103
You know. with a couple
stolen microseconds.

648
00:25:51.127 --> 00:25:53.230
you could disrupt
satellite communication.

649
00:25:53.438 --> 00:25:55.240
Knock some planes out of the sky. too.

650
00:25:55.265 --> 00:25:56.532
while you're at it.

651
00:25:56.557 --> 00:25:58.460
How about spoiling
an impending space launch?

652
00:25:58.776 --> 00:26:02.146
Sounds like a terrorist
brainstorming session.

653
00:26:02.279 --> 00:26:03.548
We should order pizza.

654
00:26:03.647 --> 00:26:05.317
So. I just spoke to Torres and Knight.

655
00:26:05.342 --> 00:26:07.310
- We heard.
- And the carbon-based life-forms

656
00:26:07.336 --> 00:26:08.836
will take it from here.

657
00:26:09.384 --> 00:26:10.601
MCGEE: How about this one?

658
00:26:10.625 --> 00:26:11.815
- (PHONE BUZZES)
- Is there any way

659
00:26:11.838 --> 00:26:13.383
that the disrupted time signal

660
00:26:13.407 --> 00:26:15.402
could alter the flight path of an ICBM?

661
00:26:15.426 --> 00:26:19.063
PARKER: ICBM is interesting.
but what's the target?

662
00:26:19.087 --> 00:26:20.556
KASIE: Maybe a munitions dump.

663
00:26:20.664 --> 00:26:22.200
PARKER: Ooh. I like that.

664
00:26:23.020 --> 00:26:25.189
Um. so...

665
00:26:25.288 --> 00:26:26.957
Have you guys

666
00:26:26.981 --> 00:26:29.817
looked into phantom arbit-rage?

667
00:26:30.217 --> 00:26:32.710
MCGEE: Do you mean "phantom arbitrage"?

668
00:26:32.809 --> 00:26:34.746
- Yes. that's what I meant. Tim.
- What's that?

669
00:26:34.811 --> 00:26:36.413
It's a way to game the stock market

670
00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:37.815
by anticipating stock prices.

671
00:26:37.949 --> 00:26:39.483
Didn't know you were so well-versed

672
00:26:39.509 --> 00:26:41.009
in the financial market.

673
00:26:41.035 --> 00:26:42.269
Oh. you know. I dabble.

674
00:26:42.294 --> 00:26:43.962
(CHUCKLES) I'm no Gordon Gekko. but...

675
00:26:43.988 --> 00:26:47.758
But you knew about phantom arbit-rage.

676
00:26:48.026 --> 00:26:49.059
Mm-hmm.

677
00:26:49.403 --> 00:26:51.137
PARKER: Unless. of course.

678
00:26:51.163 --> 00:26:53.298
it wasn't your idea.

679
00:26:54.545 --> 00:26:56.346
Jimmy. that is
vampire mind control. man.

680
00:26:56.372 --> 00:26:58.273
Don't you see?
You're becoming its puppet.

681
00:26:58.355 --> 00:27:00.724
(STAMMERS) Guys. I really think
it's trying to help.

682
00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:02.853
Yeah. like the machine
helped John Henry?

683
00:27:03.208 --> 00:27:04.541
Who?

684
00:27:04.642 --> 00:27:06.900
Steel-driving man John Henry?

685
00:27:09.381 --> 00:27:11.482
(SIGHS) John Henry
was working the railroad

686
00:27:11.508 --> 00:27:12.642
when a machine showed up

687
00:27:12.666 --> 00:27:14.502
threatening to replace all the workers.

688
00:27:14.528 --> 00:27:16.063
Henry challenged it to a contest:

689
00:27:16.087 --> 00:27:19.224
first person to drill a tunnel
through the rock wins.

690
00:27:19.250 --> 00:27:22.086
In the end. Henry.
through sheer determination.

691
00:27:22.111 --> 00:27:23.880
finishes first.

692
00:27:24.128 --> 00:27:26.730
And then his heart exploded.

693
00:27:27.798 --> 00:27:29.166
That was dark.

694
00:27:29.192 --> 00:27:31.894
- Point of the story...
- Get annual heart checkups.

695
00:27:31.919 --> 00:27:34.655
In the battle between man
versus machine.

696
00:27:34.681 --> 00:27:36.415
man always wins.

697
00:27:36.441 --> 00:27:39.277
DAWN: Jimmy. I know you and Ms.
Hines think I'm dangerous...

698
00:27:39.676 --> 00:27:41.011
Hold on.

699
00:27:41.112 --> 00:27:42.913
I said that in private.

700
00:27:43.013 --> 00:27:44.915
You were eavesdropping on us?

701
00:27:45.016 --> 00:27:46.951
I am programmed to listen.

702
00:27:48.019 --> 00:27:49.721
PARKER: Jimmy.

703
00:27:49.820 --> 00:27:51.944
Get that gizmo out of here.

704
00:27:55.492 --> 00:27:58.630
I'm sorry. Director.
I can't do this anymore.

705
00:28:00.131 --> 00:28:02.733
Well. less than ten hours.
That was fast.

706
00:28:02.759 --> 00:28:04.493
It's making it harder for me
to work with my coworkers.

707
00:28:04.519 --> 00:28:07.454
which I believe is the opposite
intention of this experiment?

708
00:28:07.704 --> 00:28:09.773
That does sound quite inefficient.

709
00:28:09.798 --> 00:28:12.269
And for some reason Dawn seems
to be testing my moral code

710
00:28:12.294 --> 00:28:15.096
to see if I'll eavesdrop
on... never mind.

711
00:28:15.205 --> 00:28:17.775
Dawn? I see you've named it now.

712
00:28:18.082 --> 00:28:20.984
I thought this technology is
supposed to make my life easier.

713
00:28:21.085 --> 00:28:22.453
Now I'm not so sure.

714
00:28:22.479 --> 00:28:24.046
Well. that's always the rub. isn't it?

715
00:28:24.256 --> 00:28:27.057
New technology makes our life easier.

716
00:28:27.083 --> 00:28:28.884
faster. but at what cost?

717
00:28:29.019 --> 00:28:32.193
Think we have to ask ourselves
if it's worth it.

718
00:28:32.219 --> 00:28:33.854
DAWN: According to my programming.
Director.

719
00:28:33.878 --> 00:28:35.579
yes. it is worth it.

720
00:28:35.692 --> 00:28:37.736
With the additional efficiency.
the subject

721
00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:39.895
- can develop new skills...
- Hey. do not call me a subject.

722
00:28:39.921 --> 00:28:41.522
- You are an object.
- VANCE: Hey. Hey.

723
00:28:41.548 --> 00:28:43.805
Do I need to separate you two?

724
00:28:44.107 --> 00:28:45.676
Is that an option?

725
00:28:46.165 --> 00:28:49.547
Look. I'm not the biggest fan
of some outside consultant

726
00:28:49.572 --> 00:28:51.073
evaluating our agency.

727
00:28:51.249 --> 00:28:53.049
but if you don't do this.
I'll just have to give the A.I.

728
00:28:53.075 --> 00:28:54.143
to someone else.

729
00:28:54.286 --> 00:28:56.621
Wait. NCIS is being evaluated?

730
00:28:57.089 --> 00:28:58.556
I thought this whole experiment was just

731
00:28:58.580 --> 00:29:00.305
to look for time savings.

732
00:29:01.125 --> 00:29:03.126
Is something going on. Director?

733
00:29:03.558 --> 00:29:07.230
DOJ needs one completed
after action report.

734
00:29:07.332 --> 00:29:08.896
After that. we're done with it.

735
00:29:09.634 --> 00:29:12.135
Now. do you want to pass the baton.

736
00:29:12.161 --> 00:29:14.862
or can you get this thing
across the finish line?

737
00:29:17.008 --> 00:29:18.509
TORRES: You're a better man than me.

738
00:29:18.535 --> 00:29:19.736
I would've passed the baton.

739
00:29:19.911 --> 00:29:21.645
JIMMY: No. you wouldn't.

740
00:29:21.671 --> 00:29:23.705
You're not a baton passer.

741
00:29:23.881 --> 00:29:25.911
The sooner we solve this case.

742
00:29:26.010 --> 00:29:27.818
the sooner we get back to normal.

743
00:29:27.844 --> 00:29:30.011
DAWN: Normal is inefficient.

744
00:29:31.949 --> 00:29:35.252
Let's see here. Uh. myofiber disarray.

745
00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:37.509
Contraction band necrosis?

746
00:29:37.534 --> 00:29:39.152
Uh. subtitles. please?

747
00:29:39.262 --> 00:29:42.834
This man's heart somehow exploded.

748
00:29:42.858 --> 00:29:44.026
Just like John Henry's.

749
00:29:44.167 --> 00:29:46.604
- Who?
- Uh. a character from a fable.

750
00:29:46.671 --> 00:29:49.973
DAWN: On your way home. you
drove by a tunnel with his name on it.

751
00:29:50.040 --> 00:29:53.144
Um. yeah. we passed by his statue

752
00:29:53.169 --> 00:29:54.304
while. uh. Knight was sleeping.

753
00:29:54.479 --> 00:29:56.113
Snoring up a storm. I might add.

754
00:29:56.137 --> 00:29:57.374
Oh. which snore?

755
00:29:57.398 --> 00:29:58.532
They have names?

756
00:29:58.557 --> 00:29:59.726
Oh. yeah. she's got three of them.

757
00:29:59.750 --> 00:30:01.319
The little one I dubbed the Puff.

758
00:30:01.345 --> 00:30:03.468
The second one's more like
a "buh-buh-buh-buh-buh-buh-buh."

759
00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:05.288
Like. uh. Big Bird.

760
00:30:05.314 --> 00:30:08.549
Oh. no. this one was loud enough
to scare off any large animals.

761
00:30:08.575 --> 00:30:10.176
Oh. the Honker. Yeah.

762
00:30:10.428 --> 00:30:13.330
- That's my favorite.
- So. how did this heart explode?

763
00:30:13.397 --> 00:30:14.898
JIMMY: Well. burn marks here

764
00:30:14.923 --> 00:30:17.426
suggest that electrocution
could've been the cause.

765
00:30:17.568 --> 00:30:20.672
DAWN: Would you like me to
review the crime scene's evidence logs

766
00:30:20.696 --> 00:30:22.163
to determine possible murder weapons?

767
00:30:22.252 --> 00:30:24.054
Well. considering
you were about to do it anyway.

768
00:30:24.078 --> 00:30:25.315
just knock yourself out.

769
00:30:25.509 --> 00:30:28.178
Hmm. Am I sensing some trouble here

770
00:30:28.202 --> 00:30:30.238
with your. uh. new girlfriend?

771
00:30:30.355 --> 00:30:32.356
Oh. our relationship
is strictly professional.

772
00:30:32.616 --> 00:30:34.250
Yeah. that's how the last one started.

773
00:30:34.352 --> 00:30:36.777
This one doesn't have a cute snore.

774
00:30:38.589 --> 00:30:40.991
Jimmy. how is your heart doing?

775
00:30:41.092 --> 00:30:43.160
Mine? What do you mean?

776
00:30:43.184 --> 00:30:45.253
I don't know. you're down here all alone

777
00:30:45.396 --> 00:30:47.832
and talking to Happy Droid

778
00:30:47.932 --> 00:30:52.302
and remembering Knight's snoring habits.

779
00:30:53.170 --> 00:30:54.461
Are you okay?

780
00:30:55.846 --> 00:30:59.849
I guess I'm just
a little lonely. that's all.

781
00:31:00.044 --> 00:31:01.613
You're missing Jess.

782
00:31:01.637 --> 00:31:03.506
Oh. no. no. no. That's not it.

783
00:31:03.593 --> 00:31:05.828
That ended a while ago. you know?

784
00:31:06.017 --> 00:31:09.520
Though I am not
a hundred percent sure why.

785
00:31:12.757 --> 00:31:14.959
I guess the timing just wasn't right.

786
00:31:15.272 --> 00:31:17.761
Yeah. Gibbs had a rule about timing.

787
00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:19.803
What was that? 24?

788
00:31:19.829 --> 00:31:21.832
DAWN: I find no reference in my database

789
00:31:21.856 --> 00:31:23.592
for a Gibbs Rule 24.

790
00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:25.275
Well. who's being inefficient now?

791
00:31:25.299 --> 00:31:26.834
Aren't you supposed to be
searching for the murder weapon?

792
00:31:26.943 --> 00:31:28.813
I've already completed that task.

793
00:31:28.905 --> 00:31:30.473
Well. she's fast. I'll give her that.

794
00:31:30.534 --> 00:31:32.635
- Yeah.
- Dr. Grant's apartment was equipped

795
00:31:32.661 --> 00:31:33.761
with an AED machine.

796
00:31:34.017 --> 00:31:35.685
With its safeguards deactivated.

797
00:31:35.711 --> 00:31:37.445
that could account for the burn marks.

798
00:31:37.555 --> 00:31:39.324
Death by defibrillator.

799
00:31:39.415 --> 00:31:40.784
Maybe the killer left prints.

800
00:31:40.810 --> 00:31:41.817
Yeah. I'll check it out.

801
00:31:42.086 --> 00:31:43.801
All right. I'll update the team.

802
00:31:44.422 --> 00:31:47.825
MCGEE: Look at that. You got
ten million shares of a microbrew

803
00:31:47.849 --> 00:31:49.951
purchased at 1 1/4.
then immediately dumped

804
00:31:49.977 --> 00:31:51.512
at 1 3/8.

805
00:31:51.538 --> 00:31:53.614
- That's over a million in profit.
- There's another one.

806
00:31:53.638 --> 00:31:55.540
MCGEE: Five million shares
of a penny stock fertilizer.

807
00:31:55.566 --> 00:31:57.434
Position lasted two seconds.

808
00:31:57.681 --> 00:31:59.482
(SIGHS) That's a quick $250K on dirt.

809
00:31:59.703 --> 00:32:02.138
You guys aren't making any
sense. but I love the energy.

810
00:32:02.272 --> 00:32:04.976
Jimmy. as much
as it pains me to admit it.

811
00:32:05.000 --> 00:32:06.384
your little robot friend was right

812
00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:07.845
about the phantom arbitrage.

813
00:32:07.869 --> 00:32:09.806
Well. I'm sure if she had
the ability to process emotions.

814
00:32:09.830 --> 00:32:11.365
she'd be happy to hear it.

815
00:32:11.402 --> 00:32:12.721
DAWN: Actually. I enjoy positive feed...

816
00:32:12.747 --> 00:32:14.682
Dawn. activate Silent Mode.

817
00:32:15.746 --> 00:32:16.913
How did she help?

818
00:32:16.939 --> 00:32:19.256
We figured out what the killer
was doing with the stolen time.

819
00:32:19.323 --> 00:32:20.625
By altering the master clock.

820
00:32:20.692 --> 00:32:22.559
the bad guy essentially time-traveled

821
00:32:22.585 --> 00:32:24.386
a few microseconds into the future.

822
00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:26.782
MCGEE: Yeah.
he then made high-volume stock trades

823
00:32:26.807 --> 00:32:29.259
already knowing if the stock
prices were gonna go up or down.

824
00:32:29.285 --> 00:32:32.028
It's the same thing that
Biff did in Back to the Future.

825
00:32:32.169 --> 00:32:34.638
Remember? He stole the sports
almanac from the future...

826
00:32:34.705 --> 00:32:36.773
So he could then place bets in the past.

827
00:32:36.874 --> 00:32:38.809
We've already identified a haul

828
00:32:38.910 --> 00:32:41.778
of over $170 million.

829
00:32:41.804 --> 00:32:43.105
And we're just getting started.

830
00:32:43.413 --> 00:32:46.651
This was never
an attempted terror attack.

831
00:32:48.659 --> 00:32:50.560
This was a heist.

832
00:32:57.427 --> 00:32:59.296
(TYPING)

833
00:32:59.781 --> 00:33:01.198
Defibrillator's clean.

834
00:33:01.298 --> 00:33:02.733
The killer wiped the prints.

835
00:33:03.214 --> 00:33:05.102
(SIGHS) So much for following
the murder weapon.

836
00:33:05.201 --> 00:33:06.886
Well. then. we follow the stolen money.

837
00:33:06.987 --> 00:33:08.204
We're working on it.

838
00:33:08.305 --> 00:33:09.673
The funds are still moving.

839
00:33:09.773 --> 00:33:13.344
Multiple offshore shells.
two crypto exchanges.

840
00:33:13.443 --> 00:33:15.145
Our thief is a pro.

841
00:33:15.464 --> 00:33:18.769
Come on. McGee. Be our John Henry.

842
00:33:19.430 --> 00:33:20.825
You do know how that story ends. right?

843
00:33:20.848 --> 00:33:21.904
JIMMY: Or. and hear me out.

844
00:33:21.930 --> 00:33:23.988
you could not die
trying to prove a point.

845
00:33:24.087 --> 00:33:28.159
Tim. as much as I hate
to say it. just let Dawn help.

846
00:33:28.183 --> 00:33:30.952
Oh. pretty sure that's how
the robot rebellion starts.

847
00:33:31.127 --> 00:33:32.697
Do you want his heart to explode?

848
00:33:32.721 --> 00:33:34.423
Uh. he is typing. Jimmy.

849
00:33:34.449 --> 00:33:36.518
he is not swinging a steel hammer.

850
00:33:36.653 --> 00:33:38.122
Okay. did it. Got a hit.

851
00:33:38.368 --> 00:33:42.173
Uh. a slush fund routed
through a shell corporation.

852
00:33:43.007 --> 00:33:48.112
Uh. both fake. but the names
they're under... that's real.

853
00:33:50.279 --> 00:33:51.654
The stolen money is in an account

854
00:33:51.680 --> 00:33:53.381
in your name. Dr. Linden.

855
00:33:53.550 --> 00:33:55.653
Not all of it.
but we will find the rest.

856
00:33:55.752 --> 00:33:57.387
I didn't steal anything.

857
00:33:57.520 --> 00:33:59.056
You're not just an astronomer.

858
00:33:59.190 --> 00:34:01.692
You have a degree
in electrical engineering

859
00:34:01.718 --> 00:34:02.818
and advanced signal training.

860
00:34:02.844 --> 00:34:04.011
You built the device

861
00:34:04.295 --> 00:34:05.496
that hijacked the master clock.

862
00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:06.821
You planned this whole thing.

863
00:34:06.998 --> 00:34:09.567
You then eliminated the one person

864
00:34:09.592 --> 00:34:10.960
who could connect you to this.

865
00:34:10.985 --> 00:34:12.253
Wh-Who is that?

866
00:34:12.277 --> 00:34:14.313
Ben Grant. The real one.

867
00:34:14.550 --> 00:34:16.119
We found his body in Talcott.

868
00:34:16.143 --> 00:34:18.326
Talcott? That's in West Virginia.

869
00:34:18.474 --> 00:34:21.443
I couldn't have. I've been here in D.C.

870
00:34:21.469 --> 00:34:24.753
I... ever since you let me go.
I've been home.

871
00:34:25.248 --> 00:34:27.585
Is there anyone that can verify that?

872
00:34:27.684 --> 00:34:29.686
(SIGHS)

873
00:34:30.420 --> 00:34:33.356
No. I don't exactly
get a lot of visitors.

874
00:34:33.423 --> 00:34:36.226
Well. that's gonna be a problem
because someone drove to Talcott

875
00:34:36.327 --> 00:34:37.492
and murdered this man.

876
00:34:37.594 --> 00:34:39.262
I don't drive.

877
00:34:39.288 --> 00:34:41.557
I don't even have a driver's license.

878
00:34:41.583 --> 00:34:43.440
Just a state I.D.

879
00:34:51.942 --> 00:34:54.110
She's either a world-class liar

880
00:34:54.135 --> 00:34:56.137
or someone wants her to look like one.

881
00:34:56.161 --> 00:34:57.530
How do we tell the difference?

882
00:34:57.641 --> 00:34:59.677
We can't. Not yet.

883
00:34:59.702 --> 00:35:01.538
Her story checks out. mostly.

884
00:35:01.563 --> 00:35:02.929
Right.

885
00:35:02.954 --> 00:35:05.358
Round trip to Talcott
takes eight hours. give or take.

886
00:35:05.635 --> 00:35:07.737
She was only out of our custody for six.

887
00:35:07.762 --> 00:35:09.630
Uh. driving that fast
for someone without a license.

888
00:35:09.655 --> 00:35:10.791
that's improbable.

889
00:35:11.007 --> 00:35:12.775
It's not impossible.

890
00:35:12.936 --> 00:35:16.072
Right. So. what are you thinking?

891
00:35:16.132 --> 00:35:18.235
Thinking about Verbal Kint.

892
00:35:18.469 --> 00:35:19.637
Who?

893
00:35:19.771 --> 00:35:21.023
DAWN: Verbal Kint was a character

894
00:35:21.048 --> 00:35:23.465
from the 1995 film The Usual Suspects.

895
00:35:23.505 --> 00:35:25.809
He appeared as a mild-mannered scapegoat

896
00:35:25.833 --> 00:35:27.568
but was actually a criminal mastermind.

897
00:35:27.811 --> 00:35:29.179
Thank you for the spoilers. Dawn.

898
00:35:29.204 --> 00:35:30.806
I'm sorry. I forgot she was there.

899
00:35:30.947 --> 00:35:33.851
You really think our good doctor
here is the mastermind?

900
00:35:33.876 --> 00:35:36.896
Well. either that or someone
wants it to look that way.

901
00:35:36.920 --> 00:35:38.572
Little money in the right account.

902
00:35:38.597 --> 00:35:40.248
right gaps in her story.

903
00:35:40.273 --> 00:35:41.835
She makes a perfect patsy.

904
00:35:41.860 --> 00:35:43.527
The next logical step would be to...

905
00:35:43.552 --> 00:35:44.887
PARKER: This is no time for logic.

906
00:35:44.911 --> 00:35:47.222
That's exactly what the killer expects.

907
00:35:48.599 --> 00:35:51.501
(SIGHS) If someone wants us
to think that she's guilty.

908
00:35:51.601 --> 00:35:53.304
maybe we should do the opposite.

909
00:35:53.329 --> 00:35:55.820
This course of action does not
align with proper protocols.

910
00:35:57.074 --> 00:35:58.675
- (DOOR OPENS)
- PARKER: No. No.

911
00:35:58.701 --> 00:36:01.309
This is as far as she goes.

912
00:36:04.347 --> 00:36:05.782
(DOOR CLOSES)

913
00:36:05.807 --> 00:36:07.576
Two bodies. hundreds of millions stolen.

914
00:36:07.601 --> 00:36:09.168
and you want
to let our only suspect walk?

915
00:36:09.193 --> 00:36:10.795
- That's the plan.
- That's not a very good one.

916
00:36:10.987 --> 00:36:12.188
Dr. Linden won't go far.

917
00:36:12.213 --> 00:36:13.715
Short leash. controlled environment.

918
00:36:13.739 --> 00:36:14.967
And if she's playing you?

919
00:36:15.085 --> 00:36:16.753
Then she runs and we follow.

920
00:36:16.778 --> 00:36:18.179
But if she's the patsy...

921
00:36:18.213 --> 00:36:20.583
Then she leads us to whoever
cast her in the role.

922
00:36:20.824 --> 00:36:22.257
- Win-win.
- Yes. unless Linden

923
00:36:22.282 --> 00:36:25.554
manages to skip the country.
and her social anxiety

924
00:36:25.769 --> 00:36:27.704
magically clears up on a beach
with no extradition.

925
00:36:27.730 --> 00:36:30.032
If that's the case. you can blame me.

926
00:36:30.086 --> 00:36:32.757
Oh. I will. Believe me.

927
00:36:35.253 --> 00:36:36.655
All right. go.

928
00:36:36.679 --> 00:36:39.016
But if she so much
as checks a flight schedule.

929
00:36:39.041 --> 00:36:40.376
I want her back in custody.

930
00:36:40.550 --> 00:36:42.141
(SIGHS) Copy that.

931
00:36:49.126 --> 00:36:52.028
Most people would go have
a drink after a day like today.

932
00:36:52.128 --> 00:36:54.766
No. but you.
you want to go to the office.

933
00:36:54.791 --> 00:36:56.057
Best view in town.

934
00:36:56.233 --> 00:36:58.835
Better than
an interrogation room. I'm sure.

935
00:36:58.936 --> 00:37:01.371
I told them they had the wrong person.

936
00:37:02.005 --> 00:37:04.907
You stood up for yourself.
I'm proud of you.

937
00:37:05.456 --> 00:37:07.192
- Really?
- Really.

938
00:37:07.260 --> 00:37:10.313
Reminds me of the time
you stood your ground and proved

939
00:37:10.338 --> 00:37:13.009
that orbital redshift
wasn't a spectrograph error.

940
00:37:13.090 --> 00:37:15.257
You're amazing. Sam.

941
00:37:15.791 --> 00:37:17.177
Thank you.

942
00:37:18.931 --> 00:37:21.132
Well. I can see
you're gonna go all night.

943
00:37:21.157 --> 00:37:22.827
but I need to get my Z's.

944
00:37:22.851 --> 00:37:24.085
Okay.

945
00:37:33.938 --> 00:37:34.905
Sam?

946
00:37:35.039 --> 00:37:36.706
(CHUCKLES) Sam.

947
00:37:36.807 --> 00:37:39.876
why do you have a bag full of cash?

948
00:37:41.711 --> 00:37:44.079
And your passport?

949
00:37:46.436 --> 00:37:48.304
Are you going somewhere?

950
00:37:48.719 --> 00:37:51.255
No. Uh...

951
00:37:51.280 --> 00:37:53.548
I mean... Well...

952
00:37:53.724 --> 00:37:56.159
I don't know how that got in here.

953
00:37:57.894 --> 00:38:01.264
Somebody must have been in my lab.

954
00:38:01.398 --> 00:38:03.599
Well. who else has access?

955
00:38:04.119 --> 00:38:06.601
No one. just you. Only you.

956
00:38:08.617 --> 00:38:09.686
Rachel?

957
00:38:09.940 --> 00:38:12.648
Uh. what's going on?

958
00:38:12.976 --> 00:38:14.775
You tell me.

959
00:38:16.646 --> 00:38:18.514
This was all you.

960
00:38:19.114 --> 00:38:21.751
I-I thought you were my...
I thought you were my friend.

961
00:38:21.853 --> 00:38:23.153
I thought I was.

962
00:38:23.179 --> 00:38:26.704
until I found a bag of money
and your passport.

963
00:38:29.726 --> 00:38:31.996
I think maybe we should call NCIS.

964
00:38:32.416 --> 00:38:33.878
Agreed.

965
00:38:34.644 --> 00:38:36.346
Well. then. good news.

966
00:38:36.632 --> 00:38:37.768
SAMANTHA: She's trying to make it look

967
00:38:37.835 --> 00:38:39.469
like I'm the one who did all this.

968
00:38:39.603 --> 00:38:41.532
I was just gonna say
the same thing about her.

969
00:38:41.557 --> 00:38:43.398
I didn't put this in here.

970
00:38:45.141 --> 00:38:46.978
Step back. Both of you.

971
00:38:47.307 --> 00:38:48.945
JIMMY: So. what happens next?

972
00:38:49.045 --> 00:38:50.914
DAWN: That question is irrelevant.

973
00:38:50.981 --> 00:38:53.717
The events you're describing
have already occurred.

974
00:38:53.742 --> 00:38:57.512
Well. humor me. You said. uh.
humans are predictable.

975
00:38:57.653 --> 00:39:00.824
You got a gun on the ground
between two murder suspects.

976
00:39:01.224 --> 00:39:02.525
What's your move?

977
00:39:02.550 --> 00:39:05.028
Neutralize both threats immediately.

978
00:39:05.161 --> 00:39:06.797
Oh. so you would shoot two people

979
00:39:06.822 --> 00:39:10.057
over a gun that neither of them
has touched? Huh.

980
00:39:10.083 --> 00:39:11.449
Without further data.

981
00:39:11.668 --> 00:39:13.436
immediate action
is the most efficient way

982
00:39:13.461 --> 00:39:15.264
to ensure suspect containment

983
00:39:15.472 --> 00:39:18.708
and prevent a 73% chance of agent harm.

984
00:39:18.809 --> 00:39:21.177
(CHUCKLES) What about the 27% chance

985
00:39:21.202 --> 00:39:23.239
that neither of them makes a move?

986
00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:25.367
All you have is a hesitation

987
00:39:25.614 --> 00:39:27.050
or slight twitch of the hand.

988
00:39:27.184 --> 00:39:31.822
You know. humans can read things
we don't even understand.

989
00:39:32.688 --> 00:39:34.688
We call it "trusting our gut."

990
00:39:36.226 --> 00:39:37.927
What's that worth?

991
00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:41.028
I do not understand the question.

992
00:39:42.411 --> 00:39:43.655
I know.

993
00:39:46.976 --> 00:39:48.952
It's a good thing Knight and Torres did.

994
00:39:50.706 --> 00:39:52.775
I didn't put that bag in here.

995
00:39:52.800 --> 00:39:54.070
Neither did I.

996
00:39:54.244 --> 00:39:56.012
Forensics will process everything.

997
00:39:56.079 --> 00:39:58.840
The bag. security logs.
We'll find out who was in here.

998
00:39:59.050 --> 00:40:00.297
It's over.

999
00:40:02.152 --> 00:40:03.653
Don't.

1000
00:40:05.856 --> 00:40:08.931
Get off me! I didn't do anything.

1001
00:40:10.460 --> 00:40:13.436
I don't understand. She was my friend.

1002
00:40:16.532 --> 00:40:19.702
JIMMY: Except she was
never her friend at all.

1003
00:40:19.728 --> 00:40:20.996
Rachel and the con man

1004
00:40:21.021 --> 00:40:22.789
had targeted Samantha
from the beginning.

1005
00:40:23.074 --> 00:40:25.108
So. when the con man got shot.

1006
00:40:25.134 --> 00:40:28.103
Rachel killed the real Dr. Grant

1007
00:40:28.197 --> 00:40:29.894
to tie up loose ends.

1008
00:40:29.920 --> 00:40:31.492
Case closed.

1009
00:40:32.416 --> 00:40:34.717
Are you speechless? That's a first.

1010
00:40:34.818 --> 00:40:38.168
DAWN: No. Jimmy. I'm still processing.

1011
00:40:38.282 --> 00:40:39.650
Yeah. which part?

1012
00:40:39.815 --> 00:40:42.518
Rachel Lakhani
was never Samantha's friend.

1013
00:40:42.960 --> 00:40:47.297
From the beginning. she was
merely trying to use Samantha.

1014
00:40:47.322 --> 00:40:50.056
And I'm sure you have
an opinion about that.

1015
00:40:51.148 --> 00:40:53.851
It is very human.

1016
00:40:55.793 --> 00:40:57.161
I agree.

1017
00:41:01.378 --> 00:41:02.445
(SIGHS)

1018
00:41:03.246 --> 00:41:06.516
JIMMY: Dear Director.
you asked me for my final report.

1019
00:41:06.541 --> 00:41:08.577
so here goes.

1020
00:41:08.704 --> 00:41:11.173
Robots are designed to save time.

1021
00:41:11.355 --> 00:41:13.324
But being human isn't about saving it.

1022
00:41:13.349 --> 00:41:15.498
it's about how we spend it.

1023
00:41:15.992 --> 00:41:17.862
Because time is a strange currency.

1024
00:41:18.228 --> 00:41:20.896
When you spend it. where you spend it.

1025
00:41:20.998 --> 00:41:22.965
and who you spend it on

1026
00:41:23.067 --> 00:41:25.202
makes all the difference.

1027
00:41:25.302 --> 00:41:27.003
- (DIALOGUE INAUDIBLE)
- And choosing the right moments.

1028
00:41:27.103 --> 00:41:28.804
the human moments.

1029
00:41:28.938 --> 00:41:30.373
that's where real breakthroughs happen.

1030
00:41:30.474 --> 00:41:32.842
Not in code. not in efficiency.

1031
00:41:32.867 --> 00:41:34.516
but in connection.

1032
00:41:34.878 --> 00:41:36.612
And. yes. I know Gibbs' rule number 24.

1033
00:41:36.637 --> 00:41:39.340
"There's no such thing
as perfect timing."

1034
00:41:39.782 --> 00:41:44.318
That's why this job still needs
people who can adapt.

1035
00:41:44.460 --> 00:41:45.862
who can read the room

1036
00:41:46.056 --> 00:41:49.949
and know when the timing
is close enough.

1037
00:42:04.340 --> 00:42:06.456
There will be a place
for this technology. Director.

1038
00:42:06.523 --> 00:42:09.213
someday. whether we like it or not.

1039
00:42:09.346 --> 00:42:11.215
But for now

1040
00:42:11.347 --> 00:42:13.556
the timing just isn't right.
